        "policy": "Income - Salaried, Hourly, Contract, Part-Time, Bonus or Casual",
        "description": "[ACCEPTED] **Employment Income:**\r\n- Employment income is received at regular intervals and set at an established amount. Ex. Salaried full-time income, long-term benefits, part-time income with guaranteed hours.\r\n- Confirmation of the Letter of Employment will be completed by the Fulfillment team.\r\n- Primary income where the employment is less than one year in a different line of work will be assessed for reasonability and sustainability by the Credit Risk Department.\r\n**Employment Income Verification**\r\nPrimary Source - Obtain 2 of the following Options:\r\n- Current LOE.\r\n- 2 Recent Paystubs (Within 60 days).\r\n- T4.\r\n- Part time guaranteed income \u2013 3 recent paystubs.\r\n- NOA \u2013 Notice of Assessment.\r\n- Most recent year end Paystub.\r\n**LOE Requirements**\r\n- Letter of Employment - within 60 days on Company letter head. *Verifying start date, rate of pay, hours guaranteed and status/job title. *Probation status is required if employment is under 6 months.\r\n**Variable/Part-time/Seasonal/Contract/Dividend Income**\r\n- Variable Income is received at inconsistent intervals and may fluctuate over subsequent pay periods. Given the inconsistency in income, the lender must obtain a minimum of two-year history.\r\n- Examples of variable income can include \u2013 seasonal, by industries that experience frequent layoffs, casual income with non-guaranteed hours, full-time contract/ part-time income with non-guaranteed hours, commission-based income, is it dependent on performance, full details to be noted.\r\n- Given the inconsistency in income a 2-year history will be required for qualification purposes. If the most recent year\u2019s income is less than the previous year, the most recent year income is to be used.\r\n- If a 2-year average is not available- more documentation may be required and will be reviewed on a case-by-case basis. Please speak to your BDM.\r\n- Consideration must be given to the sustainability of the income. ex: Is the income expected to increase/decrease or remain consistent over the foreseeable future? Does the year to date reflect the average income?\r\n**Seasonal workers- 100 % of Employment Insurance (E.I) income will be recognized for qualification purposes. ** 70% of the income must be derived from the salary paid by the company and no more than 30% should come from E.I. Variable/Seasonal Income Verification/Contract /Dividend Income.**\r\n**Primary Source:**\r\nObtain the following:\r\n- 2 recent paystubs.\r\n**Secondary Source:**\r\nObtain 2 years of one of the following:\r\n- NOA & T1 General.\r\n- T4/T4A.\r\n- Letter of Employment - on Company letter head. *Verifying start date, rate of pay, hours guaranteed and status/job title. If the above is not available.\r\nIf the borrower does not qualify based on existing policy and guidelines noted above, UnionLink may be able to make exception with additional supporting evidence to determine the appropriate qualifying income:\r\n- Lender may obtain a 3-year history of income (e.g., 2018, 2019, 2020) and an average of the previous three years may be used as qualifying income.\r\n- The additional supporting evidence as well as the reason for inconsistencies must be disclosed. (Applies: Alberta, Ontario)",
